Accessibility Research Taught

00:01:56.707 --> 00:02:00.904
Me About Building Better Content
Experiences" with Karimot Isiaka.

00:02:01.841 --> 00:02:04.643
Karimot is a product designer
with a strong interest in

00:02:04.646 --> 00:02:06.616
accessibility and inclusive design.

00:02:07.325 --> 00:02:12.041
As a past Outreachy intern at the
Wikimedia Foundation, she contributed

00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:16.511
to improving accessible reading
experiences for Wikipedia by researching

00:02:16.513 --> 00:02:21.439
accessibility needs, exploring design
solutions, and supporting the development

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:25.422
of features for users with cognitive,
visual, and motor differences.

00:02:26.081 --> 00:02:30.929
With experience across humanitarian
and SaaS platforms, she brings

00:02:30.991 --> 00:02:34.803
research-backed, user-centered
thinking to designing interfaces

00:02:34.805 --> 00:02:35.738
that work for everyone.

00:02:36.876 --> 00:02:41.269
Please feel free to add your questions
in the Zoom Q&A section, and we'll

00:02:41.271 --> 00:02:42.648
answer them at the end of the session.

00:02:43.392 --> 00:02:45.601
Use the chat to connect
with other attendees.

00:02:47.146 --> 00:02:48.733
Karimot: My name is Karimot Isiaka.

00:02:48.872 --> 00:02:53.915
I am a product designer with a
strong interest in accessibility

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:55.547
and inclusive design.

00:02:56.891 --> 00:02:58.635
Here is the topic for the day.

00:02:59.038 --> 00:03:02.985
I will be sharing my experience,
basically, and the topic is What

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.166
Accessibility Taught Me About
Building Better Content Experience.

00:03:07.619 --> 00:03:08.727
Let's dive right in.

00:03:11.786 --> 00:03:14.073
So this is the agenda for the day.

00:03:14.686 --> 00:03:19.588
I would be taking you through why I chose
the topic and sharing my experience.

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:26.604
I will talk about the research that led
to the information I'll be sharing today.

00:03:27.026 --> 00:03:33.480
I would also be talking about the pattern
behind the research findings, and also

00:03:34.401 --> 00:03:40.700
I'll be sliding in what- how we can
move from that research to practice and

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.957
how this can be applied to WordPress.

00:03:46.655 --> 00:03:47.799
So why this talk?

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:50.675
You know, they say that accessibility

00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:54.792
Is it gives access.

00:03:54.839 --> 00:04:00.178
So accessibility says give access to
everyone regardless of their ability.

00:04:00.685 --> 00:04:04.441
So this talk addresses how we
can make the access happen.

00:04:06.357 --> 00:04:08.097
So let's talk about where it began.

00:04:08.797 --> 00:04:13.302
It started when I got into
the contribution stage of the

00:04:13.328 --> 00:04:20.765
Outreachy internship, and the task
we were given was to redesign a

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.857
Wikipedia webpage, specifically
the Wikipedia community portal.

00:04:26.173 --> 00:04:29.784
So we were redesigning it for
accessibility requirement.

00:04:30.437 --> 00:04:38.883
And from designing it, what I learned
was that it was very important

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:42.200
that we pay attention to the
people that we are designing for.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:48.561
In this case, we were designing with
different accessibility considerations

00:04:48.565 --> 00:04:51.393
in mind, and that experience taught me.

00:04:52.099 --> 00:04:58.789
It moved me from, you know, designing
a webpage for accessibility requirement

00:04:59.248 --> 00:05:05.030
to realizing how much empathy, you
know, how much having empathy for your

00:05:05.342 --> 00:05:10.941
visitors, like the visitors of your
webpage, how much it shapes good design.

00:05:11.961 --> 00:05:18.006
So let's dive right in to get more
information about how that went.

00:05:19.998 --> 00:05:21.477
So the research stage.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.653
When I wanted to start working
on the project, of course, I

00:05:25.655 --> 00:05:27.769
didn't just jump into designing.

00:05:28.467 --> 00:05:31.293
I started with research.

00:05:32.399 --> 00:05:36.934
First of all, there is the project,
um, description, of course.

00:05:36.997 --> 00:05:41.286
So before we start, we've… In
the project description, I already

00:05:41.286 --> 00:05:44.844
identified what the problem
we, we are trying to solve is.

00:05:45.514 --> 00:05:50.124
We are trying to redesign a,
um, a webpage, the Wikipedia

00:05:50.152 --> 00:05:52.002
Community Portal specifically.

00:05:52.674 --> 00:06:00.260
But we are designing it to better
fit the experience of people who

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:04.154
have different accessibility needs,
whether they are visually impaired,

00:06:04.176 --> 00:06:07.847
whether they have cognitive disability,
motor impairment and the likes.

00:06:08.186 --> 00:06:11.658
So I did some background digging.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.984
I read articles, I watched videos on
how to design for people with these

00:06:15.986 --> 00:06:18.370
various access- accessibility needs.

00:06:18.526 --> 00:06:28.879
I also, um, had the… I had access
to some… I had access to looking

00:06:28.881 --> 00:06:33.941
into the real accounts of people
who have these experiences, and I'll

00:06:33.943 --> 00:06:40.215
be sharing those different personas
with you as we go on in this talk.

00:06:40.932 --> 00:06:47.960
So I looked at the example of
people who have visual impairment.

00:06:48.603 --> 00:06:50.803
So there are different
personas that you would see.

00:06:50.818 --> 00:06:54.946
Visual, it covers visual
impairment, non-visual, motor

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.608
and cognitive disabilities.

00:06:56.865 --> 00:07:02.844
And we went a little bit deeper, um,
with Stefan and Simone's experience,

00:07:02.875 --> 00:07:08.409
which you will get to see, because they
have cognitive, um, impairment in their

00:07:08.416 --> 00:07:14.793
own case, which is not as, um, popularly
discussed in the accessibility space

00:07:14.993 --> 00:07:17.275
compared to, like, the visual impairment.

00:07:19.046 --> 00:07:23.606
So the first persona we'll be
looking at is Lexie, is an online

00:07:23.614 --> 00:07:26.204
shopper with colorblindness.

00:07:26.688 --> 00:07:34.701
So he cannot, in his case, Lexie cannot…
In her case, Lexie cannot reliably

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:38.289
distinguish red, green, orange, brown.

00:07:38.606 --> 00:07:42.968
These colors that many sites
use alone to convey meaning.

00:07:43.944 --> 00:07:47.511
And she experiences some
barriers as a result of that.

00:07:47.539 --> 00:07:53.973
For instance, mandatory field, you
know, all those mandatory field.

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:59.079
Let's say you are, you are… You missed
a part… When you're filling a form

00:07:59.394 --> 00:08:04.909
and there's a part that is mandatory,
and it's, the only way you can know

00:08:04.909 --> 00:08:09.992
that it's mandatory is that they just
convey the message with like a color

00:08:09.993 --> 00:08:17.727
red, or they just used a color to out-
outline To communicate that requirement.

00:08:17.833 --> 00:08:22.962
So the barrier for her is that
the… These mandatory field errors

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:28.159
marked in red are hard for her to
spot at checkout, for instance.

00:08:28.573 --> 00:08:33.939
So when she's checking out and filling
some forms, if it's just communicated

00:08:34.192 --> 00:08:37.789
via color, she finds it very difficult.

00:08:37.832 --> 00:08:44.202
So what's works for her in this case
is that you don't rely on color alone.

00:08:44.213 --> 00:08:47.424
You pair it with icon,
labels, and patterns.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.610
Let's look at the next persona.

00:08:53.071 --> 00:08:57.043
This is Lakshmi, is blind
and he uses screen reader.

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:58.617
He's a senior accountant.

00:08:59.159 --> 00:09:03.658
So he navigates a webpage
entirely through a screen reader.

00:09:04.341 --> 00:09:10.193
So one of the… Some of the
barriers that he faces is unlabeled

00:09:10.193 --> 00:09:15.473
images, unclear form fields, and
deeply nested navigation menus.

00:09:17.598 --> 00:09:19.439
These are the barriers that he faces.

00:09:19.912 --> 00:09:26.882
So what works in his case, proper heading
structure and labeled field forms.

00:09:27.359 --> 00:09:29.870
So all of these things, they
are not just a nice-to-have.

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:32.714
We are going to look at more personas.

00:09:34.446 --> 00:09:36.377
In this case, um, here is Ade.

00:09:36.697 --> 00:09:43.731
He's a reporter with limited use of his
arm following his, his spinal cord injury.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.305
So Ade relies mostly on keyboard.

00:09:47.402 --> 00:09:50.507
Sometimes he just take…
operated with his palm and

00:09:50.507 --> 00:09:54.558
increasingly speech recognition.

00:09:55.337 --> 00:10:00.008
So he switches… basically, he switches
method depending on how tired he is.

00:10:00.615 --> 00:10:04.696
So the barrier he faces is that
when he's tabbing through a page,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:12.287
he often can't tell the elements
currently has focus since some

00:10:12.381 --> 00:10:16.355
sites lack visible focus indicators.

00:10:16.789 --> 00:10:24.682
So in his case, what works is when
pages have, you know, visual focus

00:10:24.713 --> 00:10:26.650
indicator, and here is an example.

00:10:28.184 --> 00:10:34.547
So for you to… when you are designing
a webpage, for instance, if your team

00:10:34.548 --> 00:10:41.337
is designing a webpage, you want to
ensure that for people like Ade, there

00:10:41.351 --> 00:10:48.241
is a visual indica- a focus indicator
that tells him that this is where

00:10:48.241 --> 00:10:50.839
he is at this point in the website.

00:10:51.191 --> 00:10:55.936
So let's say he has… he's on a button.

00:10:57.728 --> 00:11:02.228
He should be able to know that, yes,
I'm on this button, as opposed to the

00:11:02.239 --> 00:11:09.063
button looking the same or staying the
same as it was before he put, like,

00:11:09.284 --> 00:11:13.438
he put his mouse across that button.

00:11:13.939 --> 00:11:16.929
So this is what that looks like.

00:11:17.651 --> 00:11:20.779
This is just a button
showing you different states.

00:11:21.089 --> 00:11:24.903
From the default state to hover state,
and it's showing you, like, a different

00:11:24.933 --> 00:11:29.040
color outline for the focus indicator.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:39.630
So let's go in deep, deeper
for personas who have cognitive

00:11:39.959 --> 00:11:42.763
disabilities like dyslexia and ADHD.

00:11:42.839 --> 00:11:45.689
But before then, let's look
at the definition of both.

00:11:46.785 --> 00:11:50.865
So according to the National Institute
of Neurological Disorders and Stroke,

00:11:50.882 --> 00:11:57.664
dyslexia is a brain-based type of learning
disability that is… that specifically

00:11:57.675 --> 00:11:59.827
impairs a person's ability to read.

00:12:00.883 --> 00:12:05.711
ADHD, on the other hand, is a
developmental disorder marked by

00:12:05.738 --> 00:12:12.571
persistent symptoms of inattention,
hyperactivity, and impulsivity.

00:12:13.093 --> 00:12:18.001
So ADHD means attention
deficit hyperactivity disorder.

00:12:18.509 --> 00:12:23.046
So I just wanted to set that
before we move into the personas

00:12:23.071 --> 00:12:26.245
who experience these disabilities.

00:12:26.962 --> 00:12:29.919
Stefan struggles.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:38.643
He has ADHD and dyslexia, so he
has difficulty recognizing and

00:12:38.644 --> 00:12:43.251
sounding out words, which affects his
spelling and reading comprehension.

00:12:44.218 --> 00:12:51.008
So he also relies on text-to-speech so he
can see and hear words at the same time.

00:12:52.741 --> 00:12:56.434
So this helps him focus,
especially on long text.

00:12:57.306 --> 00:13:02.292
So it is very important to actually
have context of the type of people

00:13:02.308 --> 00:13:04.245
that may be visiting our website.

00:13:04.749 --> 00:13:08.875
If we have the context of these
different personas in mind, it helps

00:13:08.876 --> 00:13:13.235
to bring things into perspective,
and it helps us also become more

00:13:13.235 --> 00:13:14.799
intentional when we are designing.

00:13:15.394 --> 00:13:18.727
So Stefan's str- is… Some of
his struggle, he gets lost in

00:13:18.738 --> 00:13:21.551
dense, dense text with no breaks.

00:13:23.251 --> 00:13:29.129
So he also get distracted by pop-ups,
moving ads and cluttered layouts.

00:13:29.419 --> 00:13:33.288
He struggles to nav-navigate
sites with unclear structures.

00:13:33.819 --> 00:13:39.482
He misspells words while
searching, and he gets no results.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:43.113
Sites also don't respect
his brow- browser settings.

00:13:44.714 --> 00:13:50.668
So what works for him is plain
language, generous white space, visual

00:13:50.691 --> 00:13:58.492
supports like icons, simple, consistent
layouts, site maps, breadcrumb

00:13:58.524 --> 00:14:01.542
trails and search functionality.

00:14:02.181 --> 00:14:06.035
Another thing that works for
him is design that adapts to

00:14:06.047 --> 00:14:09.055
his user setting preference.

00:14:11.319 --> 00:14:14.228
Here is another persona.

00:14:14.394 --> 00:14:15.406
Here is Simone.

00:14:16.605 --> 00:14:20.703
Simone was diagnosed with dyslexia.

00:14:21.137 --> 00:14:26.738
In her case, she uses specialist
dyslexia software which reads out

00:14:26.806 --> 00:14:31.935
documents and webpages for her, and
it also helps her to read and spell.

00:14:32.765 --> 00:14:36.596
Even though the website… I mean,
the software helps her to feel more

00:14:36.597 --> 00:14:40.449
confident about her writing, she
also asks her colleagues to helps,

00:14:40.693 --> 00:14:44.895
to help her check important emails
before sending it, just in case.

00:14:45.113 --> 00:14:49.367
So this is a use case for another persona.

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:51.488
So some of her struggle.

00:14:51.498 --> 00:14:58.130
Forms take a long time to fill in, and
she often needs help checking her writing.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.760
Poor spelling, so she relies on
tools to guess what this meant.

00:15:03.145 --> 00:15:06.896
Loses concentration around moving
content banner and tickers.

00:15:08.228 --> 00:15:12.554
She struggles with dense
writing and complex language.

00:15:13.738 --> 00:15:19.323
So some of the things that work for her is
almost similar to the case of Stefan, the

00:15:19.346 --> 00:15:22.549
previous… one of the previous personas.

00:15:23.343 --> 00:15:27.974
So the save and return
functionality, reasonable timeouts.

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:35.214
This one is one of the WCAG,
that's WCAG, um, success criterion.

00:15:35.486 --> 00:15:40.984
We have to actually give reasonable
timeou- timeout for users

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:44.910
like Simon who have dyslexia.

00:15:45.266 --> 00:15:49.974
We want to… Let's say they are
filling a form and after 10 minutes

00:15:49.978 --> 00:15:53.535
of inactivity, the form would close.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:59.684
Instead of just closing the form directly,
you can, let's say two minutes before the

00:16:00.344 --> 00:16:08.150
end of 10 minutes, you let them know that
the form will be closing so they can click

00:16:08.151 --> 00:16:10.263
the option that allows them more time.

00:16:11.094 --> 00:16:18.974
So they also want, um, spell-tolerant
search and input tools that, you know,

00:16:19.987 --> 00:16:24.295
that kind of allows them to, let's
say they are spelling… Maybe they

00:16:24.298 --> 00:16:31.348
didn't get the spelling wrong, so you
can build your site in such a way that

00:16:31.348 --> 00:16:33.456
recommends the correct spelling for them.

00:16:33.885 --> 00:16:37.266
So avoid unnecessary mes-
um, motion on the page.

00:16:38.867 --> 00:16:46.275
Plain English, like you don't have to
be too complicated because for people

00:16:46.275 --> 00:16:50.660
like this, you want the content on
your website to be easy to understand.

00:16:51.185 --> 00:16:55.366
And adjustable contrast
between text and background.

00:16:55.679 --> 00:17:00.564
You can give them different option to
switching between light and dark mode.

00:17:00.809 --> 00:17:05.665
You can also add extra layers or
something that looks like what I have

00:17:05.675 --> 00:17:08.692
here, like a sepia background for your…

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:10.930
You can make that option
available for them.

00:17:11.675 --> 00:17:15.387
So also avoid justified text and italics.

00:17:16.638 --> 00:17:17.550
So let's move on.

00:17:18.315 --> 00:17:20.338
So what the research revealed.

00:17:20.418 --> 00:17:25.016
Let's look at what all of… Because
studying all of these personas and

00:17:25.016 --> 00:17:29.771
learning about them, the research
revealed a couple of things.

00:17:30.936 --> 00:17:33.402
Different people, different
barrier, one pattern.

00:17:33.445 --> 00:17:37.505
Let's, let's look at what was…
Even though they have different

00:17:37.507 --> 00:17:41.482
experiences, let's look at the
pattern that continued to show up.

00:17:42.177 --> 00:17:46.161
So across every story, all of these
people that we've just read about,

00:17:46.581 --> 00:17:50.717
the specific barriers were different,
but three things kept showing up again

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:53.427
and again, and they were the structure

00:17:55.838 --> 00:17:58.660
The things that make, um, these
three things are what actually

00:17:58.661 --> 00:18:01.515
make the page usable for them.

00:18:01.791 --> 00:18:04.626
The structure, clarity, and also control.

00:18:05.242 --> 00:18:07.892
Let's look at them in
a little more detail.

00:18:09.244 --> 00:18:13.456
So what does these three
things, what do they look like?

00:18:13.572 --> 00:18:18.625
In the case of Lakshmi, he
navigates-- she navigates

00:18:18.801 --> 00:18:21.291
rather, she navigates by heading.

00:18:21.302 --> 00:18:25.572
So all of these structures, they
are not just a nice-to-have.

00:18:26.159 --> 00:18:32.395
For someone that uses, um, screen readers,
she wants to be able to navigate well.

00:18:32.672 --> 00:18:41.545
And when your headings are not properly
cited and identified, if they are

00:18:41.545 --> 00:18:47.399
not properly structured, for someone
that uses a screen reader, she's

00:18:47.421 --> 00:18:49.823
going to come across some challenges.

00:18:49.871 --> 00:18:52.609
So she navigates by heading.

00:18:52.619 --> 00:18:58.147
You also want to ensure that you
are intentional about how you

00:18:58.147 --> 00:19:01.991
structure the pages of your website.

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:08.051
In Stefan's cage… In Stefan's case,
he needs plain language and white

00:19:08.052 --> 00:19:13.208
space to stay consistent, to stay
with the content instead of giving up.

00:19:14.892 --> 00:19:19.294
Stefan needs plain language and
white space to stay with the

00:19:19.322 --> 00:19:20.785
content instead of giving up.

00:19:21.108 --> 00:19:27.995
In the next, um, redesign that I
would be sharing with you, you're

00:19:27.996 --> 00:19:29.568
going to see how this plays out.

00:19:30.373 --> 00:19:33.458
So in Simone and Ade's
case, they need control.

00:19:33.764 --> 00:19:38.818
They want adjustable contrast, adjustable
timeout to work on their own pace.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:46.572
So all of these things are
also supported by the British

00:19:46.589 --> 00:19:49.834
Dyslexia Association's guideline.

00:19:51.324 --> 00:19:55.487
Everything that I just mentioned, the
plain language, the consistent structure,

00:19:55.725 --> 00:20:01.713
and also giving user control over
content, over how content is presented.

00:20:02.014 --> 00:20:04.599
We are going to see more as we go further.

00:20:06.417 --> 00:20:12.045
So from research to practice, how
do we apply all of these things

00:20:12.099 --> 00:20:18.504
to WordPress when we are building
websites on WordPress and when we

00:20:18.507 --> 00:20:21.058
are designing content on WordPress?

00:20:25.513 --> 00:20:28.485
So everything we've covered, the
structure, the clarity, the control,

00:20:28.518 --> 00:20:30.512
they are not abstract ideas.

00:20:30.905 --> 00:20:33.996
This translates into
real, buildable decisions.

00:20:34.553 --> 00:20:41.283
So what it looks like across… This
is how it looks like across four

00:20:41.297 --> 00:20:46.570
areas: content structure, navigation,
readability, and personalizations.

00:20:46.645 --> 00:20:48.567
Let's quickly look into that.

00:20:48.824 --> 00:20:52.302
So for content structure,
it's not just decoration.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:58.143
It is how people move through pages,
just like our previous user or

00:20:58.143 --> 00:21:03.667
persona that is visually impaired
and works with, um, a screen reader.

00:21:04.105 --> 00:21:09.080
So using real reading hierarchy
instead of just… Because

00:21:09.081 --> 00:21:12.780
sometimes when we, when we want to-

00:21:15.454 --> 00:21:17.311
Identify our headers.

00:21:17.691 --> 00:21:22.979
We sometimes make the mistake of just
making the header bold, but beyond

00:21:22.991 --> 00:21:27.549
that, we need to actually name it.

00:21:28.371 --> 00:21:31.021
We need to style it as the heading.

00:21:31.317 --> 00:21:34.792
We need to style it as the heading,
as opposed to just making it bold.

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:41.776
So if it's just a written experience
where you're writing a letter

00:21:42.013 --> 00:21:47.747
and you're making the heading
part, you know, bolder, it works.

00:21:47.922 --> 00:21:55.247
But when it comes to the, the web,
you have to really define your header

00:21:55.294 --> 00:22:02.698
and structure it properly beyond
just making it look like it or being

00:22:02.732 --> 00:22:07.372
able to structure it in a way that
the screen reader can catch it.

00:22:07.952 --> 00:22:10.612
So break content into short paragraphs.

00:22:11.023 --> 00:22:16.522
The British Dyslexia Association
also recommends roughly

00:22:16.579 --> 00:22:18.051
100 characters per line.

00:22:18.106 --> 00:22:21.671
We would also see how that applied in
the redesign project that I worked on.

00:22:22.017 --> 00:22:26.866
Use lists and short sections
instead of dense blocks of text.

00:22:28.208 --> 00:22:29.606
So let's look at it.

00:22:30.153 --> 00:22:33.069
Before the redesign, um, the Wikipedia…

00:22:33.446 --> 00:22:37.743
Sorry, yes, the Wikipedia Community
Portal has been linked here.

00:22:37.743 --> 00:22:39.566
You can always check it out later.

00:22:41.311 --> 00:22:47.767
So here is some of the screenshots of the
original layout of the community portal.

00:22:53.471 --> 00:22:54.223
Yes.

00:22:54.755 --> 00:22:55.569
Let's keep going.

00:22:55.819 --> 00:22:58.962
Can we see how dense the contents look?

00:22:59.826 --> 00:23:06.061
And this, there is also not enough white
space, especially for users like Stefan

00:23:06.062 --> 00:23:16.629
and Simone who need a lot of white space,
and they also do not learn well when

00:23:16.704 --> 00:23:21.754
there's a lot of content that is just
very, that are just very close together.

00:23:23.047 --> 00:23:24.956
So let's take a look at the redesign.

00:23:25.629 --> 00:23:32.695
In my case, l- just learning about
these users and understanding their

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:40.032
challenges, I was able to empathize with
them and also redesign the community

00:23:40.032 --> 00:23:45.889
portal in such a way that allows for
what I've heard them say works for them.

00:23:46.164 --> 00:23:51.157
Like, it's, for instance, the
British dyslexia A- Association

00:23:51.364 --> 00:23:56.515
already said that we should have
roughly 10 characters per line.

00:23:56.578 --> 00:24:02.290
I mean, s- I take that back, 100
characters per line on a webpage.

00:24:02.647 --> 00:24:06.868
So even if it will be more than
100, it will not be too much.

00:24:06.994 --> 00:24:15.311
It will not be far off that it's too long
that the- That the user or the website

00:24:15.329 --> 00:24:17.552
visitor is not able to follow along.

00:24:18.005 --> 00:24:19.925
So this is what the redesign looks like.

00:24:20.288 --> 00:24:25.752
And if you look at this section
from the original website,

00:24:26.367 --> 00:24:29.131
it was not this spaced out.

00:24:29.207 --> 00:24:30.811
Everything was joined together.

00:24:31.192 --> 00:24:37.698
So I was able to kind of categorize
this and add some spacing to each

00:24:37.908 --> 00:24:40.814
of the, to each of, like, the boxes.

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:48.404
And also in the event part…
Let's quickly go back for a bit.

00:24:48.485 --> 00:24:50.586
Look at this part that talks about event.

00:24:52.139 --> 00:24:59.109
The color contrast, when I did the test,
it was, it, it wasn't contrasting well

00:24:59.111 --> 00:25:03.473
because there's, like, a light green
background there, and there's a blue text.

00:25:03.704 --> 00:25:04.910
It was not contrasting well.

00:25:04.911 --> 00:25:09.953
But beyond that, there are two
columns saying for different

00:25:10.466 --> 00:25:13.151
things, and they are side by side.

00:25:13.398 --> 00:25:17.143
So this can… So they're
also very close together.

00:25:17.649 --> 00:25:19.070
They're a bit clumsy.

00:25:19.622 --> 00:25:23.548
And for someone who has dyslexia,
for instance, this is going to be

00:25:23.552 --> 00:25:25.233
difficult for them to navigate.

00:25:27.151 --> 00:25:29.378
So what I did was to…

00:25:29.836 --> 00:25:30.343
Yes.

00:25:30.632 --> 00:25:34.147
What I did in this part was to
make it easy because there's,

00:25:34.228 --> 00:25:38.309
on the community portal page,
there is a lot of information.

00:25:38.325 --> 00:25:44.018
So I grouped this information in such
a way that I used accordion to group

00:25:44.019 --> 00:25:50.918
the information so that when a visitor
wants to learn ab- more details about,

00:25:50.937 --> 00:25:58.021
let's say, events and projects, they can
click on, o- on it, and it brings out

00:25:58.079 --> 00:26:00.486
the details about event and projects.

00:26:00.985 --> 00:26:07.841
If they want about another section of the,
of the page, maybe the general notice,

00:26:07.856 --> 00:26:14.783
the signpost, they can always click on
the accordion, and also it brings out

00:26:14.783 --> 00:26:19.435
the details, as opposed to outlining
everything at once, and everything is

00:26:19.511 --> 00:26:26.196
in a, in like a column, in a two-column
section, and they're seeing inform-

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:30.478
two information staring at them at the
same time, and it's just so clumsy.

00:26:31.844 --> 00:26:38.811
So for another section, I also used
accordion to kind of space things out.

00:26:40.681 --> 00:26:49.339
For the footer in the original community,
um, website, the content was… The

00:26:49.371 --> 00:26:53.472
content kind of started from the
beginning of the page to the end of

00:26:53.472 --> 00:27:00.978
the page, and it was not… It was so
long, and it should be easy for… It

00:27:01.029 --> 00:27:05.765
would also be easy for them to, like,
get lost in the process of trying to

00:27:06.451 --> 00:27:10.550
fit in all of the content at once.

00:27:14.909 --> 00:27:18.352
So let's go to the next
part, the navigation.

00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:23.434
Navigation, uh, helps people
find their way, basically.

00:27:23.467 --> 00:27:28.575
So add a visit-a visible
sitemap or content index.

00:27:28.855 --> 00:27:34.541
So at the top of your page, you
can add a visible sitemap and

00:27:34.589 --> 00:27:36.675
content index that is clickable.

00:27:37.113 --> 00:27:39.870
So beyond it just being
visible, make it clickable.

00:27:40.322 --> 00:27:47.252
If they are like something that looks like
basically a content col- a content table

00:27:47.604 --> 00:27:57.722
where if a visitor wants to navigate to
a different section, instead of scrolling

00:27:57.745 --> 00:28:02.469
all the way, they can just click on it
and it takes them right to this section.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:07.347
You can also, to make the navigation
easier, keep the navigation

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:09.421
consistent a-across pages.

00:28:09.689 --> 00:28:15.617
An example of this is the menus, the
way we label menu on our webpage.

00:28:15.664 --> 00:28:23.858
On the homepage, a lot of times you will
see the About, um, you see maybe Blog,

00:28:24.515 --> 00:28:28.762
you would see Contact Us and the likes.

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:33.712
So the way it is arranged on one
page is the way it should continue

00:28:33.717 --> 00:28:35.898
to be arranged on all other pages.

00:28:36.271 --> 00:28:40.972
When you change that, you can
confuse your website visitors.

00:28:43.373 --> 00:28:44.263
Readability.

00:28:44.559 --> 00:28:49.145
This one, this particular
one cannot be overemphasized.

00:28:50.468 --> 00:28:56.750
Design is… You're not just designing
the interface to make it look good.

00:28:57.555 --> 00:29:00.903
The words, the text, they
are also part of the design.

00:29:01.171 --> 00:29:04.711
So they are not just… You're
not just designing the layout.

00:29:04.891 --> 00:29:09.753
So the, your choice of fonts and the
way the text come up on your webpage,

00:29:09.811 --> 00:29:11.040
they are very, very important.

00:29:11.045 --> 00:29:13.794
So left-align this text.

00:29:13.844 --> 00:29:15.379
Don't justify the text.

00:29:17.253 --> 00:29:24.033
This is also one of the recommendation
by the British Dyslexia Association.

00:29:24.543 --> 00:29:31.235
So avoid the use of italics because you
know that, for instance, someone with

00:29:31.501 --> 00:29:37.085
dyslexia, they find it difficult to read.

00:29:38.443 --> 00:29:42.464
So when the text also now don't
look like what they are used to,

00:29:43.087 --> 00:29:47.302
it can cause confusion for them.

00:29:47.553 --> 00:29:50.731
So we want to make sure that we
are making things as clear as

00:29:50.732 --> 00:29:58.277
possible and not, you know, trying
to make things difficult because

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:00.885
we are trying to beautify our text.

00:30:01.496 --> 00:30:04.856
So use legible sans serif for body text.

00:30:05.317 --> 00:30:10.787
Never rely on color alone to convey
meaning, as we have seen from the

00:30:10.787 --> 00:30:13.863
experiences of, of some of our personas.

00:30:14.333 --> 00:30:17.147
Avoid moving or auto-playing content.

00:30:18.061 --> 00:30:23.475
This can be distracting for
someone that ex- that experiences

00:30:23.548 --> 00:30:25.785
ADHD or that has ADHD.

00:30:26.407 --> 00:30:32.098
So in WordPress, avoid animated
hero sliders or auto-rotating

00:30:32.214 --> 00:30:34.881
carousels on content-heavy pages.

00:30:35.768 --> 00:30:37.609
That can be distracting.

00:30:38.241 --> 00:30:40.352
It can be very, very distracting.

00:30:40.966 --> 00:30:42.437
This cannot be overstated.

00:30:42.487 --> 00:30:49.660
So if they, the user now decide to play
it themselves, give them an option to be

00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:53.180
able to pause or play all these carousels.

00:30:56.402 --> 00:30:57.592
Personalization.

00:30:57.732 --> 00:31:04.738
Let users, let them, let your web page
visitors, let them shape the page.

00:31:04.833 --> 00:31:06.165
Let them not just view it.

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:08.142
So how can this happen?

00:31:08.497 --> 00:31:12.264
Let users adjust contrast,
font size and background.

00:31:13.731 --> 00:31:18.839
Respect their browser level accessibility
setting instead of overriding them.

00:31:19.137 --> 00:31:26.538
So design your website in such a
way that it accommodates for the

00:31:26.848 --> 00:31:31.854
browser setting of, of the visitor.

00:31:32.521 --> 00:31:38.476
Let it not override the setting that they
already have on their system or on their

00:31:38.476 --> 00:31:40.470
operating system or on their browser.

00:31:40.697 --> 00:31:44.462
So in WordPress, you can
use accessibility plugin for

00:31:44.473 --> 00:31:47.728
reading mode or contrast toggle.

00:31:48.808 --> 00:31:55.338
So in this next step says,
configure theme.json to respect

00:31:55.353 --> 00:32:00.147
user font size preference as a
lower lift sta-starting point.

00:32:00.295 --> 00:32:06.194
Basically, an example is
using flexible units like rem

00:32:06.311 --> 00:32:08.629
instead of fixed pixel values.

00:32:09.222 --> 00:32:17.563
So for your text size, use a flexible
unit as opposed to using a fixed

00:32:17.569 --> 00:32:26.397
pixel value whereby the, the visitor,
the website visitor is not able to,

00:32:26.403 --> 00:32:31.713
like, change the s-text size to fit
into their own prese-preference.

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:35.313
So how do we…

00:32:35.354 --> 00:32:36.940
Let's go back to where we started from.

00:32:37.701 --> 00:32:46.011
You have learnt about Lexie,
Lakshmi, Ade, Stefan, Simon, and

00:32:46.011 --> 00:32:48.816
you've seen a real redesign change.

00:32:51.907 --> 00:32:59.031
So I was able to change all of this
when I understood what they needed.

00:32:59.421 --> 00:33:08.020
So we basically moved from, "This is
the page I am designing," to, "How do I

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:12.770
design for these people?" It was also my
first… It was also my first time of,

00:33:12.791 --> 00:33:19.495
you know, redesigning a page for people
that have diverse accessibility needs.

00:33:19.818 --> 00:33:27.156
It was my first time of considering
people who have cognitive disabilities.

00:33:28.272 --> 00:33:36.284
I was used to just focusing on color
contrast, um, also looking at, yes,

00:33:36.297 --> 00:33:41.142
majorly color contrast for people with
dis- disability, um, visual impairment.

00:33:41.427 --> 00:33:48.564
Also the heading labels, you know, making
sure the heading is properly labeled.

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:55.037
But just being able to understand that
this is the way someone who has cognitive

00:33:55.057 --> 00:34:01.017
disability like ADHD and dyslexia
consume content, just being able to

00:34:02.186 --> 00:34:06.369
put myself, empathize with them, put
myself in their shoes and understand

00:34:06.407 --> 00:34:10.739
how they consume content on webpages.

00:34:11.086 --> 00:34:17.735
I was able to s- understand what their
challenges are and think of the redesign

00:34:18.426 --> 00:34:23.471
from the angle where I'm empathizing with
them, and I'm also researching solutions

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:28.109
that fit into their own experience.

00:34:28.822 --> 00:34:35.813
So I applied all of these solutions
that includes, you know, that includes

00:34:37.371 --> 00:34:47.380
using generous white space, not
justifying my text, not using carousel

00:34:47.408 --> 00:34:55.156
anyhow on my webpage, you know, and
all of, all, all of these things.

00:34:55.458 --> 00:35:06.666
And also not making sure, not using
unnecessarily long characters for my text.

00:35:07.574 --> 00:35:10.950
No, n- number of characters on your,
on my text should not be too long.

00:35:10.963 --> 00:35:14.647
It should not be too long that it's-

00:35:17.086 --> 00:35:20.492
It's hard for someone that
has dyslexia to follow.

00:35:20.978 --> 00:35:27.711
So all of this was the things that I
considered when I was redesigning the

00:35:27.722 --> 00:35:34.434
community portal, and I learnt that,
you know, compliance, my key takeaway,

00:35:34.722 --> 00:35:36.448
compliance is not the finish line.

00:35:38.302 --> 00:35:41.892
A page can pass every technical
check and still be hard to use.

00:35:41.896 --> 00:35:49.739
For instance, you can run an, a, an
automatic accessibility check on a

00:35:49.739 --> 00:35:55.578
webpage, and it tells you, "Oh, there's
contrast issue here. There is… Alt text

00:35:55.578 --> 00:36:04.133
is missing here." But it does not really
tell you… It maybe in a case of the

00:36:04.155 --> 00:36:10.432
Wikipedia community portal page, it may
not really tell you that the content are

00:36:10.458 --> 00:36:13.699
clumsy and they are very packed together.

00:36:14.034 --> 00:36:20.531
And for someone like Stefan and
Simon, they would find it hard

00:36:20.946 --> 00:36:24.135
to actually read the content.

00:36:24.163 --> 00:36:30.679
They would find it hard to follow the
content structure of that type of page.

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.673
So compliance is not the finish line.

00:36:34.134 --> 00:36:41.074
Even after the technical checks, you
also want to consider the experience

00:36:41.198 --> 00:36:46.607
of the people, these type of personas
with different accessibility needs.

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:51.094
So structure, clarity, and control,
they matter as much as access.

00:36:52.204 --> 00:36:55.538
So these are the patterns that
show up again and again across

00:36:55.808 --> 00:37:01.318
all of this disability, across all
of these different disabilities.

00:37:01.844 --> 00:37:07.375
So another takeaway for me is:
small specific changes, they count.

00:37:07.575 --> 00:37:08.760
They really, really count.

00:37:09.052 --> 00:37:14.361
So adding hierarchy, plain language,
respecting user search settings.

00:37:14.921 --> 00:37:20.526
None of these requirements
needs a full rebuild.

00:37:20.615 --> 00:37:24.150
None of all of this needs a full rebuild.

00:37:24.231 --> 00:37:30.978
It's just making small, small changes
from time to time, and before you

00:37:30.978 --> 00:37:40.380
know it, you are making the experience
of a user that might be experiencing

00:37:41.006 --> 00:37:44.877
or that might have a type of
disability that you're not aware of.

00:37:45.754 --> 00:37:49.837
You're making that type of
user ex- user's experience.

00:37:49.848 --> 00:37:55.100
You're improving it just by paying
attention to all of these tiny details.

00:37:56.916 --> 00:37:58.775
So three things to check this week.

00:38:00.933 --> 00:38:02.321
Check your heading structure.

00:38:02.752 --> 00:38:06.682
Are you using real H2s, H3s and the likes?

00:38:07.497 --> 00:38:13.995
All these different heading types,
are you using them or bold text

00:38:13.995 --> 00:38:18.370
styled to look like the real header?

00:38:19.403 --> 00:38:26.408
Check if that's what you're doing on
your webpage or you're using bold text.

00:38:27.057 --> 00:38:34.392
Does it just look like it or it
is actually a real header or it

00:38:34.397 --> 00:38:36.355
has a real heading structure?

00:38:37.286 --> 00:38:43.877
Turn off auto-rotating carousels or
moving content on content-heavy pages.

00:38:44.962 --> 00:38:50.923
Again, we found out that this can
be really distracting for different

00:38:50.962 --> 00:38:58.562
users that have dyslexia or ADHD,
but they are not the only ones.

00:38:58.652 --> 00:39:06.495
Even users who have autism, they
can find this very, um, triggering.

00:39:07.027 --> 00:39:12.779
So don't auto-rotate cont--
um, carousel content, videos,

00:39:12.801 --> 00:39:15.055
images, sliding up and down.

00:39:15.075 --> 00:39:18.894
It can be very… It can make the
user experience very difficult.

00:39:19.183 --> 00:39:23.009
So you want to pay attention when
you are designing your web content.

00:39:23.721 --> 00:39:27.279
Zoom your site to three hundred
percent and see what breaks so

00:39:27.279 --> 00:39:28.513
that you can start fixing it.

00:39:28.567 --> 00:39:28.975
Okay?

00:39:30.498 --> 00:39:35.167
These are important links that
you also want to check out when…

00:39:35.663 --> 00:39:45.631
Just to learn more as you build
better web content experiences for

00:39:46.142 --> 00:39:49.502
the users that visit your webpage.

00:39:49.734 --> 00:39:55.692
And also, as you continue to build
websites on WordPress, you want to

00:39:55.710 --> 00:40:01.765
learn more about how you can make the
web experience of your users better.

00:40:03.690 --> 00:40:05.267
Oh, thank you for joining me.

00:40:05.321 --> 00:40:07.377
I hope you had a great time.

00:40:14.254 --> 00:40:15.734
Gary: Thanks very much, Karimot.

00:40:15.817 --> 00:40:17.495
Yeah, that was wonderful.

00:40:17.649 --> 00:40:21.834
Um, joining you live from
Nottingham, Robin Hood.

00:40:21.961 --> 00:40:25.779
He can't make it today because
his internet connection is bad,

00:40:25.844 --> 00:40:30.798
but, uh, it's really nice to, uh,
join you, and, uh, thanks very

00:40:30.799 --> 00:40:32.464
much for the going through that.

00:40:32.508 --> 00:40:40.116
Um, it's really good to see not just
you're talking about, um, obviously we

00:40:40.116 --> 00:40:44.764
need to cater for people with visual
disabilities, but also talking about

00:40:44.768 --> 00:40:47.663
people with, uh, motor disabilities.

00:40:48.131 --> 00:40:50.447
And, uh, I have to say, I feel seen.

00:40:50.448 --> 00:40:51.393
I've got autism.

00:40:51.393 --> 00:40:52.691
I've got ADHD.

00:40:53.136 --> 00:40:57.853
Um, I haven't got dyslexia, but I've got
something similar called Irlen syndrome,

00:40:57.853 --> 00:41:01.948
which means I have trouble reading, and
I wear these overlays on my glasses.

00:41:01.948 --> 00:41:07.033
And, um, so just to say the, the
difference between the before and after

00:41:07.040 --> 00:41:13.481
with your redesign, uh, it's so much more
relaxing to see that, uh, that website.

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.025
It looks so much better.

00:41:15.046 --> 00:41:17.538
So, um, yeah, well, well done on that.

00:41:17.582 --> 00:41:20.731
Um- Thank you, Gary … someone was
asking, uh… Oh, you're welcome.

00:41:20.734 --> 00:41:24.558
Uh, someone was asking, is the
redesign now live of that page?

00:41:26.837 --> 00:41:27.602
Karimot: Is the what?

00:41:28.272 --> 00:41:31.575
Gary: Is the redesigned
page, uh, is that live now?

00:41:33.277 --> 00:41:34.529
Karimot: No, it's not live.

00:41:35.218 --> 00:41:35.356
Is it not live?

00:41:35.356 --> 00:41:35.366
Mm-hmm.

00:41:35.366 --> 00:41:36.354
No, it's not live.

00:41:36.394 --> 00:41:40.903
But I, I think that, you know,
it's a big organization and their,

00:41:41.125 --> 00:41:44.811
um, processes can be, um, in…

00:41:45.686 --> 00:41:50.168
Their design decisions, because it was an
internship that I did, so- Mm. A couple

00:41:50.174 --> 00:41:55.957
of changes that I recommended… Okay,
I think one change that I recommended

00:41:55.957 --> 00:42:01.553
that I didn't mention in the video was
the placement of… So for users that

00:42:01.572 --> 00:42:09.570
are logged out, there is a particular
icon that was initially in between

00:42:09.965 --> 00:42:16.648
the, um, log-in and I think the-
there are, like, two primary buttons.

00:42:17.005 --> 00:42:20.651
So that particular icon
was in between them.

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:26.815
So I recommended during my redesign
that it be moved to the side, because

00:42:26.815 --> 00:42:28.680
they don't belong to the same category.

00:42:28.979 --> 00:42:32.537
And by the time I actually
resumed the internship, I saw

00:42:32.538 --> 00:42:34.259
that it, it has been moved.

00:42:34.865 --> 00:42:40.769
And so even though this is, like, a
very simple detail, it's can go a long

00:42:40.806 --> 00:42:43.338
way in improving the user experience.

00:42:43.456 --> 00:42:51.387
So when it comes to, like, accessibility
and just ensuring that, um, the different

00:42:51.428 --> 00:42:55.144
people with accessibility needs are
considered, the little things matter.

00:42:55.155 --> 00:42:58.524
You don't have to, um,
change everything at once.

00:42:58.564 --> 00:43:00.423
You can do it a step at a time.

00:43:00.781 --> 00:43:02.441
It's an ongoing process.

00:43:02.714 --> 00:43:03.116
Yeah.

00:43:04.278 --> 00:43:04.793
Gary: Absolutely.

00:43:04.793 --> 00:43:08.526
Well, I hope that they, they
follow through with your other

00:43:08.526 --> 00:43:12.126
recommendations, 'cause it, it, it's,
it makes a big difference there.

00:43:12.612 --> 00:43:16.495
Um, and I've just, uh, I've got,
got loads of questions here.

00:43:16.499 --> 00:43:21.072
Um, just one, one, one really good
question is that, um, yeah, we've

00:43:21.075 --> 00:43:26.209
talked about some users are struggling
with, uh, pop-ups and moving items.

00:43:26.799 --> 00:43:31.609
Uh, but marketing teams are always
really keen on that because it, you know,

00:43:31.834 --> 00:43:34.387
to drive engagement and conversions.

00:43:34.864 --> 00:43:41.693
I'm just wondering how we can advocate
for, um, for clarity and, and, uh,

00:43:41.698 --> 00:43:46.653
white space when, when the businesses
are insisting on, you know, let's be

00:43:46.655 --> 00:43:52.517
intrusive, let's distract people with
this advert, this pop-up, um, 'cause

00:43:52.519 --> 00:43:54.073
they want to hit those revenue goals.

00:43:54.089 --> 00:43:58.973
How can we, um, how can we,
uh, persuade them otherwise?

00:44:00.189 --> 00:44:00.469
Karimot: Yeah.

00:44:00.687 --> 00:44:06.840
I think that, um, you come with
facts, especially, um, whoever is

00:44:06.861 --> 00:44:09.246
proposing that, you come with facts.

00:44:09.281 --> 00:44:14.549
You can conduct, like, some user
research with the people who are being

00:44:14.553 --> 00:44:17.706
affected by this type of design style.

00:44:18.102 --> 00:44:23.327
And because whether you like it or
not, there are thousands of… hundreds

00:44:23.327 --> 00:44:27.011
of thousands of people across the
world who experience different forms

00:44:27.011 --> 00:44:34.724
of, um- impairment or disability, and
you're counting them out when you…

00:44:34.738 --> 00:44:36.128
It also affects your business.

00:44:36.139 --> 00:44:41.915
So you can come from the angle of, you can
come from the angle of a business case.

00:44:42.459 --> 00:44:47.990
Because- Mm. And you also have to
say that whatever you're proposing,

00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:52.559
they still get to see it, but they
just see it in a different way.

00:44:52.869 --> 00:44:58.905
Because I have also, even though I've not
been diagnosed with, um, dyslexia or ADHD,

00:44:59.192 --> 00:45:05.151
when I visit a website and everything is
moving at the same time, I start feeling,

00:45:05.437 --> 00:45:07.777
like, very uncomfortable, and I leave.

00:45:07.826 --> 00:45:11.849
I don't get to complete the task that
I went there for in the first place.

00:45:12.128 --> 00:45:20.763
So if I was supposed to maybe, um, s-
if I'm supposed to be converted, maybe

00:45:20.777 --> 00:45:25.459
if I, maybe if that was not the case,
I would have been one of their, maybe

00:45:25.459 --> 00:45:31.177
I would have signed up to their mailing
list or been able to consume whatever

00:45:31.183 --> 00:45:33.245
it is that they wanted to market to me.

00:45:33.494 --> 00:45:37.999
But because I have already left the
webpage, they've already… I'm one

00:45:38.028 --> 00:45:41.713
of many people that they've lost.

00:45:42.090 --> 00:45:46.734
So you need to, whoever is proposing
that to their, to their marketing

00:45:46.739 --> 00:45:48.751
team, you need to come with facts.

00:45:48.781 --> 00:45:52.243
You need to do your research
so that you're not just talking

00:45:52.299 --> 00:45:55.991
based on, um, what you feel.

00:45:56.342 --> 00:45:57.122
So come with- Mm

00:45:57.122 --> 00:46:01.444
facts and n- your numbers so that they
can see that there's also a business

00:46:01.447 --> 00:46:03.501
case from what you're proposing.

00:46:04.918 --> 00:46:05.164
Gary: Yeah.

00:46:05.164 --> 00:46:08.793
The previous talk, uh, talked about
business cases and the amazing

00:46:08.795 --> 00:46:14.226
amount of, um, uh, return on in-
investment that accessibility can have.

00:46:14.233 --> 00:46:15.755
So yeah, that's really good to hear.

00:46:15.756 --> 00:46:21.480
And, um, uh, someone's asking as
well, do you have, uh, go-to sources?

00:46:21.483 --> 00:46:26.021
Do you have existing sources for your
accessibility personas, or do you

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:27.646
think it's better to build your own?

00:46:29.549 --> 00:46:30.039
Okay, yes,

00:46:30.211 --> 00:46:30.828
Karimot: there are some.

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:32.226
Okay.

00:46:32.519 --> 00:46:40.441
So some, the personas that I actually
highlighted during my talk, they

00:46:40.445 --> 00:46:47.120
are actually, like, on… You can
find some of them on the gov.uk.

00:46:47.657 --> 00:46:48.318
Mm-hmm.

00:46:48.318 --> 00:46:50.961
And if you have… Okay, I
think the slides are here.

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:53.003
They've shared the slides
of my talk already.

00:46:53.230 --> 00:46:58.491
You can als- you can actually
read the interviews and real-life

00:46:58.492 --> 00:47:00.951
experiences of these personas.

00:47:01.272 --> 00:47:05.109
So instead of building yours,
you can use them as references.

00:47:05.447 --> 00:47:11.841
And you can also use Google to just search
the experiences of people who have talked

00:47:11.841 --> 00:47:18.279
about their own personal, you know, life
experience on how they navigate the web

00:47:18.693 --> 00:47:21.664
and just digital interfaces in general.

00:47:21.843 --> 00:47:26.486
So you don't have to build yours, but if
there are people that you know, you can,

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:33.457
um, get in contact with them and ask that
you learn about, that you're interested

00:47:33.457 --> 00:47:34.856
in learning about their experiences.

00:47:34.865 --> 00:47:39.054
But in my case, the personas I
cited during my talk, they actually,

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:43.537
um, I didn't know them in person,
but they were interviewed by

00:47:43.573 --> 00:47:45.989
these platforms and they were…

00:47:46.649 --> 00:47:48.958
It allowed me to actually
empathize with them.

00:47:49.095 --> 00:47:53.187
One of them, I was able to watch his
video where he actually shared his

00:47:53.223 --> 00:47:58.516
own experience, so I heard from his…
It was like a firsthand experience

00:47:58.518 --> 00:48:02.525
just watching him talk about his
experience, how he reads through the

00:48:02.527 --> 00:48:05.514
web, and how he consumes information.

00:48:05.807 --> 00:48:06.242
Yeah.

00:48:07.902 --> 00:48:08.633
Gary: No, that's excellent.

00:48:08.642 --> 00:48:12.184
So, you know, there's so
much material out there.

00:48:12.212 --> 00:48:17.316
Without having to come up with your own
personas, you can still find lots of

00:48:17.316 --> 00:48:23.021
information out there and, um, you know,
as you say, come up with some, um, some

00:48:23.021 --> 00:48:29.786
empathy and some, um, some ideas about
all the different challenges people face.

00:48:29.806 --> 00:48:31.452
So yeah, that's brilliant.

00:48:31.511 --> 00:48:33.923
Well, thank you very much, uh, Karimot.

00:48:34.066 --> 00:48:37.641
Uh, we've, uh, run out of
time- Yeah … but, uh, thanks

00:48:37.641 --> 00:48:38.881
very much for your talk today.

00:48:38.934 --> 00:48:44.480
Um, and thank you for attending
this session with Karimot Isiak.

00:48:45.149 --> 00:48:49.055
You can continue the conversation
in Slack or on social media

00:48:49.059 --> 00:48:56.893
using #WPA11YDay and #WPAD2026.

